} However, since I ultimately want to run this code on a high power computing cluster I want to avoid using std::random_device() as it is risky for systems with little entropy. So instead I want to create an initial random number generator and then jump it forward a large amount for each of the threads. I have been attempting to do this with the Xoroshiro256+ PRNG (I found some good implementation here: https://github.com/Reputeless/Xoshiro-cpp ). Something like this for example: XoshiroCpp::Xoshiro256Plus prng(42);  // properly seeded prng
