answers.body:
I believe that critical section is the cause of the problem. Consider taking all critical sections outside the parallelized loop and running them after the parallelization is over. Try this: #pragma omp parallel shared(a,pos)
{
#pragma omp for private(id2,k,r12,richtung,a_local) 
for(id=0; id <numb; ++id){
    for(id2=0; id2<id; id2++){
        for(k=0;k<3;k++){
            r12 += sqrt((pos[id][k]-pos[id2][k])*(pos[id][k]-pos[id2][k]));
        }
        for(k =0; k<3;k++){
            richtung[k] = (-1.e10)*(pos[id][k]-pos[id2][k])/r12;
            a[id][k] += richtung[k]/(((r12)*(r12))+epsilon);
            a_local[k]+= richtung[k]/(((r12)*(r12))+epsilon)*(-1.0);
        }
    }
}
}
for(id=0; id <numb; ++id){
    for(id2=0; id2<id; id2++){
        for(k=0;k<3;k++){
            a[id2][k] += a_local[k];
        }
    }
} Critical sections will lead to locking and blocking. If you can keep these sections linear, you'll win a lot in performance. Notice that I'm talking about a syntactic solution, which I don't know whether it works for your case. But to be clear: If every point in your series depends on the next one, then parallelizing is not a solution for you; at least simple parallelization using OpenMP.

