I have a parallelized for loop that looks like this: #pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0;i<N;i++) {
    InnerFunction();
} The problem is that InnerFunction needs to utilize and dynamically resize heap memory data containers, such as a vector. Ie, InnerFunction looks something like this: void InnerFunction() {
    vector<double> vec;
    //and then vec is subsequently resized as part of the function operation, which is being omitted here for brevity
} InnerFunction does not know in advance how big the vector needs to become, which is why it can't use std::array. Based on testing, I believe that the function is experiencing a performance problem (ie, slowdown) because the inner loop is constantly allocating and releasing heap memory. So instead, I rewrote the function to do this: vector<vector<double>> outervec;
outervec.resize(omp_get_max_threads());

#pragma omp parallel for
